.TH AXPORTS 5 "2008-Feb-04" Linux "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
.I /etc/ax25/axports
\- AX.25 port configuration file.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
.I axports
is an ASCII file that contains information about each of the physical AX.25
ports that are to be used.
When dealing with an AX.25 utility such as
.BR call ,
it takes an argument that is the port name.
This port name is a reference to the line within
.B axports,
which has that name as its first argument.
The information on each line contains enough information to bind
the command to a particular physical AX.25 interface, this binding
is done by matching the callsign on the line in
.B axports
with the callsign of the port set by
.B kissattach.
.LP
The lines within
.I axports
must either be a comment line, which starts with a # in the first column,
or a port description in the following format, each field being delimited
by white space:
.sp
.RS
name callsign speed paclen window description
.RE
.sp
The field descriptions are:
.sp
.RS
.TP 14
.B name
is the unique identifier of the port.
This is the name given as the port argument of many of the AX.25 support
programs.
This is
.I not
in any way related to actual device identities, just unique
.TP 14
.B callsign
the callsign of the physical interface to bind to.
.TP 14
.B speed
this is the speed of interface, a value of zero means that no speed will be
set by kissattach(8).
.TP 14
.B paclen
is the default maximum packet size for this interface.
.TP 14
.B window
the default window size (aka MAXFRAME) for this interface.
.TP 14
.B description
a free format description of this interface, this field extends to the end
of the line. This field may contain spaces.
.RE
.LP
The special port name
.B loop
denotes a virtual loopback port with no physical interface behind it.
It is used together with
.BR ax25netd (8):
an AX.25 service (ax25d, axspawn, ...) that binds the callsign of the loop
port becomes reachable as a virtual station on the AGWPE loop port (255),
bridging between local clients.  The speed, paclen and window fields are
ignored for loop ports.
.PP
On systems where the AF_AX25 sockets are provided by the userspace AGWPE
shim of
.BR ax25 (3)
instead of the kernel, the AGWPE port number is no longer the position of
the entry in this file: the shim resolves the
.B name
against the "port information" reply of the AGWPE server
.RB ( ax25netd ).
An entry whose
.B name
matches the upstream name reported by the server selects the first
channel of that upstream; a
.B :N
suffix on the
.B name
(e.g.\&
.IR agwpe-direwolf2:4 )
selects channel N of that upstream, i.e.\& the (N+1)-th radio interface
of the upstream in question: the name before the colon picks the
upstream, the suffix the channel inside it.  This is how the channels
of one radio server (one upstream in
.BR agwpe.conf (5))
are addressed.  When the server's port list is not reachable, the
position of the entry is used as the upstream index, so the port number
is position * 16 + channel.
.PP
Ports that are served by the AGWPE shim are conventionally named with an
.B agwpe-
prefix (e.g.\&
.IR agwpe-direwolf2 ).
The
.B name
is the interface identity that routing daemons and digipeater programs
see on the wire, so the prefix keeps AGWPE ports distinguishable from
kernel AX.25 interfaces and makes the backend of an entry obvious from
the configuration alone.  This matters once both backends are present on
one system (see below).  The prefix is a pure namespace marker: when the
entry is resolved, it is stripped before the remaining name is matched
against the upstream name in
.BR agwpe.conf (5),
which carries no prefix
.RB ( agwpe-direwolf2
is the interface name for the upstream
.IR direwolf2 ).
.PP
Ports served by a
.B WAMPES
node are named
.BI wampes: interface
\-\- the part before the colon says which node, the part after it which of
that node's own AX.25 ports the traffic leaves by.  The node part is
resolved on this side and never reaches WAMPES; the interface part travels
as the port prefix WAMPES understands
.RI ( "connect hfb:DB0AAA-8" ),
the same one an operator types at an RMNC or XNET.
.PP
One entry per WAMPES interface rather than one per node, because this file
refuses duplicate callsigns and every WAMPES interface has a callsign of its
own anyway.  The callsign in the entry is what an outgoing connection uses as
its source unless the program binds one itself.  Where the node listens is
named in
.BR wampes.conf (5),
one node per line.  That file is also what makes an entry a WAMPES entry: a
port belongs to a WAMPES node when the name before the colon appears there,
so nothing is guessed from the spelling and no name is reserved.  The backend
is therefore chosen per port, at
.BR bind (2),
and no environment variable is needed;
.B AXSOCK_BACKEND
remains only as an override.
.PP
A name whose node is not in
.BR wampes.conf (5)
is refused here, before any socket is opened; an interface the node does not
have is refused by the node, and
.BR connect (2)
fails with ENODEV.  A name whose node is configured but which has no entry of
its own is accepted and the node's own entry is used \-\- the node then routes
the call rather than leaving by a named port, and a line on standard error
says so once.
.PP
On platforms with a native kernel AX.25 stack (Linux) the AGWPE shim is
not built by default and AF_AX25 sockets go to the kernel unchanged.  If
libax25 was built with the shim as well
.RB ( --enable-agwpe ),
the
.B AXSOCK_BACKEND
environment variable selects the backend for a whole process:
.B kernel
passes AF_AX25 and packet sockets through to the OS,
.B agwpe
routes them through the shim, and
.B wampes
hands them to a WAMPES node.  Without the variable the choice is made
once per process: on Linux a single probe asks whether the kernel
answers AF_AX25 (kernel module loaded); if it does the kernel is used,
otherwise AGWPE.  On platforms without a native stack (macOS, BSD) the
backend is always
.B agwpe
and AXSOCK_BACKEND is ignored.
.PP
The per-process switch is a stop-gap.  socket(2) must return a file
descriptor before the callsign \-\- and therefore the port \-\- is known
at bind(2)/connect(2) time, so the backend cannot be derived per port
today.  The planned replacement is socket caching: socket() hands back a
placeholder descriptor, bind() resolves the callsign against
.I axports
and the device field of the matching entry (the
.B agwpe-
prefix marks an AGWPE port, anything else a kernel port) and dup2()s
the real descriptor (kernel socket or AGWPE socketpair) over the
placeholder, keeping the descriptor number visible to the application
stable.  That removes AXSOCK_BACKEND altogether and lets kernel,
AGWPE and WAMPES ports coexist in one process without an environment
variable.
.PP
The WAMPES backend already works this way.  Its sockets change hands at
.BR bind (2),
the first moment the port is known: whoever made the descriptor lets go of it
and the number the application holds does not change, so kernel, AGWPE and
WAMPES ports are usable side by side in one process today.  After
.BR connect (2)
it costs nothing at all:
the descriptor the application holds becomes the connection itself, so
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR poll (2)
and
.BR close (2)
reach the kernel with nothing in between.
.SH FILES
.LP
/etc/ax25/axports
